#{[17592186198122 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{\\neg } K \\\\ K \\lor  M \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\end{array}}\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198123 :question/problem "According to the disjunctive syllogism inference rule, what is missing from the series of statements below?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{l}{x} \\text{ is divisible by } {3} \\text{ or } {x} \\text{ is prime} \\\\ {x} \\text{ is not prime} \\\\ \\hline \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{[ m a t h ] x [ / m a t h ] i s d i v i s i b l e b y [ m a t h ] 3 [ / m a t h ] .}}\\end{array}}\n$$"] [17592186198124 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{l}P \\Rightarrow  \\mathrm{\\neg } Q \\\\ P \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\mathrm{\\neg } Q \\\\ Q \\lor  R \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A A}}\\end{array}}\n$$\nWhich of the following statements complete the above proof?\n\n1. $R$\n2. $\\lnot R \\lor Q$\n3. $\\lnot Q$"] [17592186198125 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{l}\\text{If } {y} \\text{ is prime, then } {y} \\text{ is not divisible by } {3} \\\\ {y} \\text{ is divisible by } {3} \\\\ \\hline ∴ {y} \\text{ is not prime} \\\\ {y} \\text{ is prime or } {y \\geq 3} \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A A A A A A}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198135 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A∪C|= {1, 2, 3, 5},; B∪C|= {1, 2, 3, 4},; A∩C|= {1},; B∩C|= {2},\n$$\ncalculate $(A \\cap B) \\cup C$ and\n$(A \\cup B) \\cap C$."] [17592186198136 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A\\times B =\\{(0,0),(0,1)\\}, \\quad A\\times C = \\{(0,0),(0,2)\\},\n$$\nwhat are $A \\times (B \\cup C)$ and $A \\times (B \\cap C)$?"] [17592186198138 :question/problem "What is the meaning of the notation $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{4}{n^3}\\right) = 0\n$$ expressed in symbolic form?"] [17592186198143 :question/worked-solution "A *null sequence* is a sequence that converges to zero:\n\n- Sequence I is a null sequence. By dividing the numerator and denominator by $n^2$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)a_{n}|= lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{n}{n^{2} + 1}\\right); = lim_(n → ∞)((\\left(\\frac{n}{n^{2}}\\right))/((\\frac{n^{2}}{n^{2}} + \\frac{1}{n^{2}}))); = lim_(n → ∞)((\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right))/((1 + \\frac{1}{n^{2}}))); = \\frac{0}{1 + 0}; = 0✓\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is *not* a null sequence since\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)b_{n}|= lim_(n → ∞)\\sqrt{n + 1}; = ∞; \\ne 0.\\cdot\n$$\n- Sequence III is null. Notice that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{8}{9}$.\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to zero:\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)c_{n}|= lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)^{n}; = 0✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186198147 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0.\n$$"] [17592186198147 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nWriting this in words, we have:\n\n> We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n$:\n\n> Solving this inequality for $n$, we get\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & < ε \\\\ 1 & < ε\\sqrt{n} \\\\ 1 & < nε^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{ε^{2}} & < n \\\\ n & > \\frac{1}{ε^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Therefore, we choose $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}$.\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\mid |= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}; < \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1/ε^{2}}}; = ε.\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0.\n$$"] [17592186198158 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186198161 :question/problem "List the elements of the set $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\left\\{ x\\in \\mathbb R \\: :\\:  \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{2}\\right) = 0  \\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186198162 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{9-x^2=0 \\mid x \\in \\Bbb Q \\}\n$$\n\nGiven the set $A$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mid A \\mid = 2$\n2. $\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & \\mathbb{Z}\\end{vmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{vmatrix}A \\cup \\mathbb{Z} & = & \\mathbb{N}\\end{vmatrix}$"] [17592186198162 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a finite set and a countably infinite set is countably infinite.\n\nAlso, note that\n\n- $A =\\{-3, 3\\}$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$, and\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{Z}$ are countably infinite with the same cardinality.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set $A$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$.\n- Statement II is false. The set $A$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$. On the other hand, $\\mathbb{Z}$ is infinite with $\\mid \\mathbb{Z}|=\\aleph_0$. So,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}A & =2 \\neq \\aleph_0 = & \\mathbb{Z}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n- Statement III is true. The set $A$ is finite, while $\\mid \\mathbb{Z} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. As a result, $\\mid A \\cup \\mathbb{Z} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. On the other hand, we know that $\\mid \\mathbb{N} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. So,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}A \\cup \\mathbb{Z} & = \\aleph_0 = & \\mathbb{N}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186198164 :question/problem "Describe the set $$\n\\displaystyle S=\\left\\{\\ldots, -36, -6, -1, -\\dfrac1{6}, -\\dfrac1{36}, ...\\right\\}\n$$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = -\\dfrac16$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*"] [17592186198171 :question/worked-solution "A set $A$ is *countably infinite* (or *denumerable*) if\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & \\mathbb{N}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nA set is *countable* if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $A$ has infinitely many elements.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle A|= {(-2)^{n}∣n ∈ N}; = {-2, 4,-8, 16,-32…}\n$$\ncan be represented as a (one-way infinite) sequence. So, it's countably infinite (or denumerable).\n- Statement III is true since any countably infinite set is also countable.\n- Statement IV is false since statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186198172 :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\mid \\: \\cos x =1 \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{Q}\n$$\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $A \\times B$ is uncountable.\n2. $A \\times B$ is finite\n3. $A \\times B$ is countably infinite"] [17592186198175 :question/problem "What is the meaning of the notation $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (\\sqrt n - n) = -\\infty\n$$ expressed in symbolic form?"] [17592186198178 :question/problem "Using the formal definition, prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186198178 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall M < 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: (4-n^2) \\lt M\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\n> We need to show that for any real $M < 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $4-n^2 < M$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $M$.\n\nFirst, notice that $4-n^2 < 0$ for any\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nSo, we solve the inequality $4-n^2 < M$ for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 - n^{2} & < M \\\\ -n^{2} & < M - 4 \\\\ n^{2} & > 4 - M \\\\ n & > \\sqrt{4 - M}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $4-n^2 < M$, it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{4-M}$.\n\nNotice that, since $M < 0$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4 - M} > \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN\\geq 3\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $M < 0$. Let's choose some natural number\n> $N > \\sqrt{4-M}$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $a_{n}|= 4 - n^{2}; < 4 - (\\sqrt{4 - M})^{2}; < 4 - 4 + M; = M$.\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186198186 :question/problem "Write down a formal definition of the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{3n^2-1}{n^2+2}\\right) = 3.\n$$"] [17592186198191 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1.\n$$"] [17592186198191 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, we simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a_{n} - L & &= & \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} + 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} + \\frac{4^{n} - 10}{4^{n} - 10} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n} + 4^{n} - 10}{4^{n} - 10} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - \\frac{9}{4^{n} - 10} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid \\frac{9}{4^{n} - 10} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\right| = \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{4^{n} - 10} & < ε \\\\ 9 & < ε(4^{n} - 10) \\\\ \\frac{9}{ε} & < 4^{n} - 10 \\\\ 10 + \\frac{9}{ε} & < 4^{n} \\\\ \\log_{4} (10 + \\frac{9}{ε}) & < n \\\\ n & > \\log_{4} (10 + \\frac{9}{ε})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} - (-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right) > \\log_4(10)\\approx 1.66\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid |= \\frac{9}{4^{n} - 10}; < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition,\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid \\mid < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} = -1.\n$$"] [17592186198199 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> $4-mn$ is even if $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $m$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$ integer and let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$ integer.\nThen,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m= \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\, \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n= \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,\n$$\nfor some $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{integers a and b}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$\n\nBy substituting the above into $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}}$, we can show that\n$4-mn=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$\nwhere $c$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{an integer}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nTherefore, since $4-mn$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{is 1 mm tt}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $4-mn$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{odd}} {\\phantom{}}$."] [17592186198200 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> $m^2 + n + 1$ is odd if $m$ and $n$ have the same parity.\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\nLet $m$ and $n$ be integers. We consider the following cases:\n\n**Case 1**. If $m$ is even and $n$ is even, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}\n$$\nfor some $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nBy substituting the above into $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$, we can show that\n$m^2 + n + 1 = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$\nwhere $c$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nSince $m^2 + n + 1$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m^2 + n + 1$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\n**Case 2**. If $m$ is odd and $n$ is odd, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}\n$$\nfor some $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nBy substituting the above into $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$, we can show that\n$m^2 + n + 1 = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$\nwhere $c$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nSince $m^2 + n + 1$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m^2 + n + 1$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$.\n\nTherefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $m^2 + n + 1$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{WXYZ}} {\\phantom{}}$ for any integers $m$ and $n$ that have the same parity."] [17592186198201 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\textrm{is odd and} n \\textrm{is even} \\Rightarrow m - n \\textrm{is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is odd and $n$ is even. Then, $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the oddness of $m$ and the evenness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand $n=2b$ into our expression $m - n$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle m - n|= (2a + 1) - (2b); = 2a + 1 - 2b; = (2a - 2b) + 1; = 2\\underbrace{(a - b)}_{c} + 1.\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=a - b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm - n = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m - n$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=a - b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $m - n|= 2\\underbrace{(a - b)}_{c} + 1; = 2c + 1$\n> which is one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $m - n$ is odd."] [17592186198204 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2p$, where $p$ is an integer.\n- An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2q + 1$, where $q$ is an integer.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nmandnhave the same parity⇒3m - 7n + 2is even\n$$\n\nIf $m$ and $n$ are of the same parity, then either both are even, or both are odd. Thus, it suffices to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(m, nare both even)∨(m, nare both odd)⇒3m - 7n + 2is even\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $m$ and $n$ are integers of the same parity and state that we'll proceed using cases.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers of the same parity. We consider the following cases:\n\nFirst, we consider the case when $m$ and $n$ are both even.\n\n> Case 1. If $m$ and $n$ are even, then $m=2a$ and $n=2b$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $3m - 7n + 2|= 3(2a) - 7(2b) + 2; = 6a - 14b + 2; = 2(3a - 7b + 1)$.\n> Let $c=3a-7b+1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> So, $3m-7n+2 = 2c$, which means that it is even.\n\nNow, we consider the case when $m$ and $n$ are both odd.\n\n> Case 2. If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 3m - 7n + 2|= 3(2a + 1) - 7(2b + 1) + 2; = 6a + 3 - 14b - 7 + 2; = 6a - 14b - 2; = 2(3a - 7b - 1).\n$$\n> Let $c=3a-7b-1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> So, $3m-7n+2 = 2c$, which means that it is even.\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $3m-7n+2$ is even for any integers $m$ and $n$ of the same parity."] [17592186198208 :question/worked-solution "We need to prove the implication\n\n$$\n13 \\mid N \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the statement in terms of congruences:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $13 \\mid N \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad N \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:13)$.\n\nNow, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n> Substituting the expression for $N$ into the congruence, we get\n> $10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nNow, we need to show that this implies\n\n$$\n10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}≡0 \\mid (mod13)\n$$\n\nThe idea is as follows:\n\n- We add $13ka_0$ for some integer $k$ to the left-hand side. This will give an expression that's also congruent to zero.\n- The value of $k$ that we pick should give a multiple of $10a_0$ when combined with the existing $a_0$ term. This allows us to factor $10$ from the left-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, note that\n\n$$\na_0+3\\cdot 13 a_0 = a_0+39 a_0 = 40a_0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Now, without changing the congruence, we can add $39a_0$ to the left-hand side since it's congruent to $0$ modulo $13{:}$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} + 39a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + 40a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod13) \\\\ 10(10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0}) & ≡0 & (mod13)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we cancel out the factor of $10$.\n\n> Since $10$ and $13$ are coprime, we can use a division rule for congruences and cancel out the factor of $10$ to get\n> $10^{n - 1}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod13)$.\n\nFinally, we can write down that conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation means that\n> $13 \\mid \\big(10^{n-1}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10a_{2} + a_{1} + 4a_0 \\big)$.\n\n**Note:** All the transformations we performed above also work in reverse, which means that the right-to-left implication is also true.\n\nThe obtained equivalence statement is known in arithmetic as the rule of divisibility by $13{:}$\n\n> *\"A number is divisible by $13$ if and only if the sum of four times the last digit and the number formed by the rest of the digits is divisible by $13$.\"*"] [17592186198209 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> Notice that the expression $4^{2n-3}+ 5$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 2$.\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $3$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $3{:}$\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 4^{2n-3}+ 5 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Working in modulo $3$, we have the following:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 4^{2n - 3} + 5 \\mid ≡(1)^{2n - 3} + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡1 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡3\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)$, as required."] [17592186198211 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. If $m +3n$ is odd, then $m$ is even or $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$m \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} {\\phantom{}} \\, \\, \\, \\text{and} \\, \\, \\, n \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} {\\phantom{}} \\, \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 3n \\, \\, \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{for any}} {\\phantom{}} \\,$\n\nIf $m$ and $n$ are $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}},\n$$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{integers}}$.\n\nSubstituting $m = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}} \\, \\, \\, \\text{and}\\, \\, \\, n = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$ into the expression $m +3n$, we can show that $m +3n= \\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$, where $c$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$\n\nTherefore, since $m +3n$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{is one more}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m +3n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid black 1pt]{\\phantom{even}}$."] [17592186198213 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(m-1)n^2 \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\neg\\left((m-1)n^2\\textrm{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow (m-1)n^2 \\textrm{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even and $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for conjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n¬(mis even andnis odd) &= ¬(mis even∧nis odd) \\\\\n&= ¬(mis even)∨¬(nis odd) \\\\\n&= mis odd∨nis even \\\\\n&= mis odd ornis even\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (m-1)n^2 \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is odd, or $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $m$ is odd, then $m=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (m - 1)n^{2}|= (2a + 1 - 1)n^{2}; = 2an^{2}; = 2\\underbrace{(an^{2})}_{b}.\n$$\n> Let $b=an^2$. Since $a$ and $n$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2\\underbrace{(an^{2})}_{b}; = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (m - 1)n^{2}|= (m - 1)(2a)^{2}; = 4(m - 1)a^{2}; = 2\\underbrace{(2(m - 1)a^{2})}_{b}.\n$$\n> Let $b = 2(m-1)a^2$. Since $a$ and $m$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $(m - 1)n^{2}|= 2\\underbrace{(2(m - 1)a^{2})}_{b}; = 2b$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $(m-1)n^2$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd."] [17592186198214 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7.6\\begin{vmatrix}- & 2.18 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 7.5 & -\\end{vmatrix}2.0\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.6 - 2.18$ is approximately $5.5$."] [17592186198215 :question/worked-solution "To estimate how much Rick has grown during this period, we have to estimate the difference $5.11-4.43$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5.11\\begin{vmatrix}- & 4.43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 5.0 & -\\end{vmatrix}4.5\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{5} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Rick grew by approximately $0.5$ feet during this period."] [17592186198216 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 a + 2 b = - 2 \\\\ 4 a - 3 b = 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{44}}},$\nthe second by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{44}}},$ and add the equations."] [17592186198217 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 p + 5 q = 7 \\\\ 3 p - 2 q = - 12\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{44}}},$\nthe second by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{44}}},$ and add the equations."] [17592186198218 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 3 y = - 7 \\\\ - 3 x + 4 y = 8\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [17592186198219 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 3 y = 3 \\\\ 5 x + 4 y = 5\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [17592186198220 :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4x - 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186198221 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 9.17\\begin{vmatrix}- & 5.4 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 9.0 & -\\end{vmatrix}5.5\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 8\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 10\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{9} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $9.17 - 5.4$ is approximately $3.5$."] [17592186198225 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 6 \\\\ y &=2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComment on the consistency of this system, the corresponding number of solutions, and its geometric interpretation."] [17592186198226 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A \\cap C = \\{1,2,4\\}, \\quad B\\cap D = \\{0,3\\},\n$$\nwhat is $(A \\times B) \\cap (C \\times D)$?"] [17592186198228 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1\n$$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t \\\\ y &=8t, \\\\ z &=2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"] [17592186198229 :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1\n$$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane."] [17592186198238 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b8/b872242423692a20a9d50658c99d9e8cee7c9b2737fb96592bc2bc35b4f72c32.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) = x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$"] [17592186198241 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198242 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j},$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$"] [17592186198242 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}\n$$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) = - 4x(x^2+y^2).\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-8xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n=\n8xy + (-8xy) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s\n$$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186198553 :question/problem "Find the solution $(a, b)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $a$:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 a + 7 b = - 1 \\\\ 5 a + 6 b = 4\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186198556 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = x, y = 2x, y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = \\frac{6}{x}.\n$$\nA transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n${\\mathbf{T}}^{- 1} : ( x , y ) \\to  ( \\hspace{0.167em} \\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A}} \\hspace{0.167em} , \\hspace{0.222em} x y ) ,$\nwhat could be the missing expression?"] [17592186198557 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $x$:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 5 y = 9 \\\\ - 11 x - 9 y = - 20\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186198558 :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$"] [17592186198558 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186198580 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only."] [17592186198581 :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{6} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186198589 :question/problem "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n$$\n\\displaystyle - {\\displaystyle \\frac{13}{5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\bbox[1pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{5}}\n$$"] [17592186198590 :question/problem "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{23}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{- 7}{\\bbox[1pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}}\n$$"] [17592186198595 :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{6} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186198597 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c34b74efe54d09b19ef4232ccae88db83a26a8f1ee18d8078771ed6a8b0e3f27.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le 4, \\frac{3π}{4} \\le v \\le \\frac{4π}{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$."] [17592186198607 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}12xy^{2}zdzdydx\n$$?"] [17592186198608 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=\n$$"] [17592186198609 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186198611 :question/problem "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}24xy\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186198612 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$"] [17592186198613 :question/problem "![](images/01/0185149b5af7fc3c8d7b69be6bef4aa6364d3a8f27a3c9730dfdedeca72926f9.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}5{x}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186198625 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"] [17592186198636 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186198636 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+2+1=3.\n$$"] [17592186198640 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186198642 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-2y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186198642 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2y)=2x, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2(0) & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186198654 :question/problem "The lamina $$\n\\displaystyle D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis."] [17592186198656 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f: R^{2} → R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin (x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\ 6x_{1} - 7x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin 1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}(x - \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [17592186198686 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}8 x + 5 y = - 7 \\\\ 7 x + 4 y = - 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186198723 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\Rightarrow  B \\\\ A \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198724 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}R \\lor  T \\\\ \\mathrm{\\neg } T \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198729 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\Rightarrow  B \\\\ \\mathrm{\\neg } B \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198733 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\land  B \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198734 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\\\ B \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198735 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\Rightarrow  B \\\\ B \\Rightarrow  C \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198740 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}B \\Rightarrow  C \\\\ A \\Rightarrow  B \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198747 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}A \\land  \\mathrm{\\neg } B \\Rightarrow  \\mathrm{\\neg } C \\\\ A \\land  \\mathrm{\\neg } B \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198748 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{\\neg } C \\\\ ( A \\Rightarrow  B ) \\Rightarrow  C \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198760 :question/problem "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$a_{n} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $ε > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(ε)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, we notice that $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n{{field-1}}\nfor any natural $n \\ge 1$. With that in mind, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid a_{n}|= \\mid \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}|= \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$ for $n$:\n\n$25$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$25$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}"] [17592186198797 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\""] [17592186198808 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = N, B = {x ∈ R∣\\sin x = 0}\n$$\nGiven the sets $A$ and $B$ above, complete the following reasoning about their union:\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-1}} and $B$ is {{field-2}}, we have that $A∪B$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186198809 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = N, B = {(-2)^{n}∣n ∈ N}\n$$\nGiven the sets $A$ and $B$ above, complete the following reasoning about their union:\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-1}} and $B$ is {{field-2}}, we have that $A∪B$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186198812 :question/problem "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$a_{n} = \\frac{1}{2n + 1}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $ε > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(ε)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, we notice that $\\frac{1}{2n + 1}$\n{{field-1}}\nfor any natural $n \\ge 1$. With that in mind, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid a_{n}|= \\mid \\frac{1}{2n + 1}|= \\frac{1}{2n + 1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$ for $n$:\n\n$2$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n$4$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}"] [17592186198816 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = {a, b, c}, B = N\n$$\nGiven the sets $A$ and $B$ above, complete the following reasoning about their Cartesian product:\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-1}} and $B$ is {{field-2}}, we have that $A \\cdot B$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186198817 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = N, B = {x ∈ R∣\\sin x = 0}\n$$\nGiven the sets $A$ and $B$ above, complete the following reasoning about their Cartesian product:\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-1}} and $B$ is {{field-2}}, we have that $A \\cdot B$ is {{field-3}}."] [17592186198821 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{\\neg } M \\\\ E \\lor  M \\\\ \\hline ∴ \\hspace{0.222em} \\bbox[2pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{E}}\\end{array}\n$$\nWhich of the following is the missing part of the logical inference rule above?"] [17592186198831 :question/problem "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$a_{n} = \\frac{4}{2n - 5}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $ε > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(ε)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, notice that $\\frac{4}{2n - 5} > 0$\nfor any natural\n{{field-1}}.\nSo, for {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{4}{2n - 5}|= \\frac{4}{2n - 5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < ε$ for {{field-3}}:\n\n$4$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n$2$ {{field-6}} {{field-7}}"] [17592186198849 :question/problem "The notation $$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$ means the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\n$ε > 0$,\n{{field-2}}\n$N ∈ N$,\n{{field-3}}\n$n ∈ N$,\n{{field-4}}\n$⇒ \\mid \\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1} -$\n{{field-5}}\n$\\mid$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186198863 :question/problem "Consider the following sequence that converges to $L = \\frac{1}{2}$:\n\n$a_{n} = \\frac{2n - 1}{4n - 3}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $ε > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(ε)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid < ε$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, simplifying $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$2n - 1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nNotice that {{field-3}}\nfor any natural $n \\ge 1$. With that in mind, we have\n\n$2n - 1$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} - L \\mid < ε$ for $n$:\n\n$2n - 1$ {{field-6}} {{field-7}} {{field-8}} {{field-9}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$1$ {{field-10}} {{field-11}}\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{2n - 1}{4n - 3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < ε\n$$ for all natural numbers $n \\ge N$."] [17592186198909 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - 7 y = 2 \\\\ - 4 x + 8 y = 8\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the first equation by\n{{field-1}},\nthe second by {{field-2}},\nand add the equations."] [17592186198910 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}8 x + 7 y = - 5 \\\\ 3 x - 8 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the first equation by\n{{field-1}},\nthe second by {{field-2}}, and add the equations."] [17592186198911 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 12 w + 3 z = 15 \\\\ 7 w - 2 z = - 8\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $z$, multiply the first equation by\n{{field-1}},\nthe second by {{field-2}}, and\nadd the equations."] [17592186198912 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 c - 5 d = 23 \\\\ 13 c - 2 d = 21\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $d$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $d$, multiply the first equation by\n{{field-1}},\nthe second by {{field-2}}, and\nadd the equations."] [17592186198914 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - 3y &=-3 \\\\ - 2x + y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}."] [17592186198929 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 - 2x \\\\ y &=-2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nSelect the correct options in the following reasonong.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}."] [17592186198931 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle P = {1, 2}, Q = {3}, R = {2}, S = {2, 3}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the statement below:\n\n$\\mid (P \\cdot Q)∪(R \\cdot S)|=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mid (P∪R) \\cdot (Q∪S)|=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$(P \\cdot Q)∪(R \\cdot S)$ {{field-3}} $(P∪R) \\cdot (Q∪S)$"] [17592186198932 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A \\cdot C|= {(1, 3), (1, 4), (2, 3), (2, 4)},; B \\cdot C|= {(2, 3), (2, 4), (4, 3), (4, 4), (6, 3), (6, 4)},\n$$\nwhat is $(A∖B) \\cdot C$?"] [17592186198937 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=6x + 5 \\\\ y &=5x + 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}."] [17592186198938 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=4 + 2y \\\\ 3y &=9 - 12x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}."] [17592186198945 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198948 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x + y \\le 3, 0 \\le y - x \\le 2}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y$\nand\n$v = y - x$,\nthe integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy\n$$\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as"] [17592186198953 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)6xdV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x, y) ∈ D, 0 \\le z \\le π - x - y}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xy$-plane enclosed between the coordinate axes and the line $y = π - x$ in the first quadrant."] [17592186198954 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{x}^{x + 1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x + y}24xydzdydx\n$$?"] [17592186198958 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4}\\cos x + 4yzdydzdx\n$$?"] [17592186198961 :question/problem "Consider the type I region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x, y) ∈ D, xy^{2} \\le z \\le 1 + 2x + y}$,\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xy$-plane. Then,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV =\n$$"] [17592186198962 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{85}{2}\\right)}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$"] [17592186198967 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdf7befa116a17a18bec24fbd13bc70569911b68f89ee3422faf2ea2826f9b68.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 - 2x}f(x, y, z)dzdydx\n$$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x$, then $z$, and then $y$. The integration region $R$ is shown above."] [17592186198968 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f06c2d69c18e088e5323533840b1fc5a6f939d3f3391506b80da2635728eef4.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sin x}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1 - y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx\n$$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $x$, then over a region $D$ in the $yz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above."] [17592186198969 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8ac5fd57665f292d8a013f47aaf20226944315bb1f74695e895dca40332b28c6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186198970 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186198972 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [17592186198975 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/78/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{105}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{15}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186198976 :question/problem "The triple integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dzdydx\n$$\nis defined over the type I region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 1, x \\le y \\le 2x, 0 \\le z \\le 1 + x + y}$.\nWhat is $a + b$?"] [17592186198993 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=2t, \\\\ z &=t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186198994 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{27}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1\n$$ and the line $\\frac{x-3}{3}=y-1=z-1.$"] [17592186198995 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+z & =1\\,⟹\\,z=1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+((1-2x)-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(-2x)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1.\n$$"] [17592186198996 :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x^{2}}{8} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1\n$$ and the plane $-y - z = 2$ onto the $xy$-plane."] [17592186198997 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 3 y = 12 \\\\ 3 x - 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"] [17592186198998 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1\n$$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=1-t, \\\\ z &=t+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186199016 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\n$$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"] [17592186199454 :tutorial/content "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina. \n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly."] [17592186199476 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)"] [17592186199491 :tutorial/content "A thin rod's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same moment as the original rod. \n\nAnalogous to the case of point masses, the center of mass of a thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin."] [17592186199500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186199511 :tutorial/content "Consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2} \\qquad \\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nLet's compare these fractions using common denominators.\n\nNotice that ${\\color{red}3}$ is *not* a multiple of ${\\color{blue}2}$. However, we can create a common denominator by taking the product of the denominators.\n\nIn this case, our common denominator will be\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}3} = 6\n$$\n\n- To put $\\dfrac12$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac13$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 \\qquad\\dfrac26\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the first fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac36 > \\dfrac26\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12 > \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186199512 :tutorial/content "Whenever two denominators have a common factor greater than $1$, the most efficient way to find a least common denominator is to look at the denominators' multiples.\n\nFor example, consider the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}6} \\qquad \\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}9}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${\\color{blue}6}$ and ${\\color{red}9}$ have $3$ as a common factor. This suggests that we can create a common denominator by looking at their least common multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $6{:}\\qquad 6,12,{\\color{purple}{18}},24,\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9{:}\\qquad 9,{\\color{purple}{18}},27,36,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, our least common denominator will be ${\\color{purple}{18}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac26$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{6 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{6}{18}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac59$ over a denominator of $18$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{9} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{9 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nSo, we now have the following two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18} \\qquad\\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nFrom here, it's easy to see that the second fraction is larger. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18}< \\dfrac{10}{18}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac26 < \\dfrac59\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186199547 :tutorial/content "To complete the picture, let's prove the main theorem of this lesson:\n\n*The maximum value of a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n*The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$*\n\n**Note:** We'll show only the first part (about the maximum value) since a similar argument can be used to prove the second part (about the minimum value).\n\nLet $A$ be a real $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix corresponding to a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin.$ As a result, there must be an *orthonormal* matrix $P$ such that \n\n- $D=P^{-1}AP=P^TAP,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\n- The diagonal entries of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2 \\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,$ where $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = \\lambda_1.$\n\n- The columns $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\mathbf{u}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{u}_n$ of $P$ form an orthonormal set of unit eigenvectors of $A.$ So, $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}} = \\mathbf{u}_1.$\n\nTake $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y.$ Since, $P$ is orthonormal, we have\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\|P \\mathbf y \\| = \\|\\mathbf y\\|\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf x^T\\!A\\mathbf x = (P\\mathbf y)^T\\!A(P\\mathbf y) = \\mathbf{y}^T (P^T\\!AP) \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}^T D \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n- $\\|\\mathbf x\\| =1 \\Longleftrightarrow \\|\\mathbf y\\| =1$ for all $\\mathbf y$ such that $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y$\n\n- Restricted to the unit sphere of $\\Bbb R^n,$ the images of the quadratic forms $Q(\\mathbf x)$  and $Q'(\\mathbf y)= \\mathbf y^T D\\mathbf y$ coincide: \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1\\}= \\{\\mathbf y^TD\\mathbf y: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf y\\|=1\\}  \\subset \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any unit vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={Q}^{′}(y) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{1}{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{2}{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{n}{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{y}_{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,‖y{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that  that \n\n$$\nM = \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{y} \\| = 1 \\} \\le \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, if we take $\\mathbf{e}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 0 & \\ldots  & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^T$ then $Q(P\\mathbf{e}_1) = Q'(\\mathbf{e}_1) = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ So, we conclude that $M = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathbf{u}_1 = P\\mathbf{e}_1$ we have that \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(\\mathbf u_1) =  \\mathbf e_1^T D \\mathbf e_1 = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.\n$$ \nTherefore, the maximum value is attained at $\\mathbf u_{\\textrm{max}}.$"] [17592186199561 :tutorial/content "Recall that any infinite subset of a countably infinite set is countably infinite. So, we have the following result concerning intersections of countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The intersection of two countably infinite sets is either finite or countably infinite.*\n\nWe also have the following result concerning unions of countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The union of two countably infinite sets is countably infinite.*\n\nThis property can be generalized to the union of any number of countably infinite sets and even to the union of *countably many* countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The union of countably many countably infinite sets is countably infinite.*\n\nWe can express this result as follows:\n\n> *If $\\mid A_i \\mid = \\aleph_0$ for all natural $i$, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\bigg \\mid \\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i \\bigg \\mid = \\aleph_0.\n$$*\n\nIf we combine \"fewer\" sets (e.g., a finite number), or if some of the $A_i$'s are finite, the resulting union must also be countable."] [17592186199567 :tutorial/content "Given two finite, non-overlapping sets of cardinalities $m$ and $n$, the (disjoint) union of these sets has cardinality $m+n$.\n\nFor example, to compute $2+2$, we can take $2$ objects and add $2$ more objects. As a result, we get $4$ objects:\n\n$$\n\\large \\underbrace{\\square \\, \\square}_{2} + \\underbrace{\\square \\, \\square}_{2} = \\underbrace{\\square \\, \\square \\, \\square \\, \\square}_{4}\n$$\n\nThis is how we learn to add natural numbers in the first place.\nNow, using this analogy, we can define rules of arithmetic involving $\\aleph_0$.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The union of a finite set and a countably infinite set is countably infinite. In other words, for any finite $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle n + \\aleph_0 = \\aleph_0 + n = \\aleph_0.\n$$\n- The union of two countably infinite sets is countably infinite. So,\n$\\aleph_0 + \\aleph_0 = \\aleph_0$.\n- The union of a finite or countable collection of countably infinite sets is countably infinite. So,\n$\\underbrace{\\aleph_0 + \\aleph_0 + \\ldots + \\aleph_0 + \\ldots}_{\\textrm{countably many times}} = \\aleph_0$.\n- The Cartesian product of a finite set and a countably infinite set is countably infinite. In other words, for any finite $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle n \\cdot \\aleph_0 = \\aleph_0 \\cdot n = \\aleph_0.\n$$\n- The Cartesian product of two countably infinite sets is countably infinite. So,\n$\\aleph_0 \\cdot \\aleph_0 = \\aleph_0$.\n- The Cartesian product of finitely many countably infinite sets is countably infinite. So,\n$\\underbrace{\\aleph_0 \\cdot \\aleph_0 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot \\aleph_0}_{\\textrm{finitely many times}} = \\aleph_0$."] [17592186199580 :tutorial/content "To show that a statement is true, we might need to break the proof up into several cases. This method is sometimes called **proof by cases**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's prove the following proposition:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nThe integers partition naturally into even and odd numbers, and these two cases cover all possible integers.\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z = \\big\\{0, \\pm 2, \\pm 4, \\ldots\\big\\} \\cup \\big\\{\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 5, \\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it suffices to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(nis even)∨(nis odd)⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $n$ is an integer and state that we'll proceed using cases.\n\n> *Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:*\n\nFirst, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> ***Case 1.*** *If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $n^2-3n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $n^{2} - 3n|= (2a)^{2} - 3(2a); = 4a^{2} - 6a; = 2(2a^{2} - 3a)$.\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - 3a$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means that it is even.*\n\nNow, we consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n> ***Case 2.*** *If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn = 2a + 1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2-3n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle n^{2} - 3n|= (2a + 1)^{2} - 3(2a + 1); = (4a^{2} + 4a + 1) - 3(2a + 1); = 4a^{2} - 2a - 2; = 2(2a^{2} - a - 1).\n$$\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means it is even*\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> *Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-3n$ is even for any integer $n$.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and full proof."] [17592186199588 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following proposition.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{mn \\text{is even}}_{P(m,n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}}_{Q(m,n)}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is even}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even or $n$ is even,\" we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis even ornis even) \\mid ⇔¬(mis even∨nis even); ⇔¬(mis even)∧¬(nis even); ⇔mis odd∧nis odd; ⇔mis odd andnis odd\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $m \\text{is odd and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are odd.\n\n> *If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then*\n> $m = 2a+1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b+1$,\n> *where $a$ and $b$ are integers.*\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $mn$ and show it is odd by writing it as one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle mn|= (2a + 1)(2b + 1); = 4ab + 2a + 2b + 1; = 2\\underbrace{(2ab + a + b)}_{c} + 1.\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a+b\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nmn = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $mn$ is indeed one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=2ab+a+b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $mn|= 2\\underbrace{(2ab + a + b)}_{c} + 1; = 2c + 1$,\n> *which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $mn$ is odd.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and its full proof."] [17592186199590 :tutorial/content "Let $A$, $B$, and $C$ be subsets of a universal set $U$.\n\n- The **associative** properties of union and intersection state the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (A∪B)∪C|= A∪(B∪C); (A∩B)∩C|= A∩(B∩C)\n$$\n- The **commutative** properties of union and intersection state the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle A∪B|= B∪A; A∩B|= B∩A\n$$"] [17592186199605 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the null geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that for $a_n$ is larger than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4$ when $n={\\color{red}{19}}$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{\\color{red}{19}} = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^{\\color{red}{19}} \\approx 0.000\\,451 > \\varepsilon.\n$$\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}$, we have that $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,4{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{20}|= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{20} \\approx 0.000301 < ε✓; a_{21}|= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{21} \\approx 0.000200 < ε✓; a_{22}|= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{22} \\approx 0.000134 < ε✓; a_{23}|= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{23} \\approx 0.000089 < ε✓; ⋮\n$$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $\\varepsilon$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,1\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{23}}\n$$\n\nIndeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{22} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{22} \\approx 0.000134 > ε\\cdot \\\\\na_{23} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{23} \\approx 0.000089 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{24} \\approx 0.000059 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{25} \\approx 0.000040 < ε✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a null sequence is that *no matter how small* $\\varepsilon$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $\\varepsilon$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $\\varepsilon$, i.e.,\n\n$$\nN= N(\\varepsilon)\n$$\n\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence is null relies on finding $N(\\varepsilon)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons."] [17592186199607 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence once more:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a formal proof that $a_n$ is null:\n\nFirst, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nmeans (using formal notation) that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by writing this in words:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n\nEarlier in the lesson, we showed that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, if we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nthen $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nUsing this result, we continue the proof:\n\n> *Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}$.*\n\nWith that in mind, we show that $\\varepsilon$ is an upper bound of $\\mid a_n \\mid$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq N{:}\n$$\n\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\mid |= \\frac{1}{n^{2}}; < (1)/((1/\\sqrt{ε})^{2}); = \\frac{1}{1/ε}; = ε\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0.\n$$*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down our formal proof."] [17592186199608 :tutorial/content "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\n, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n> *Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}$.*\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\mid |= \\frac{1}{n^{2}}; < (1)/((1/\\sqrt{ε})^{2}); = ε\n$$\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0.\n$$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof contains no details about how a suitable $N$ is found. For simple sequences, this is usually worked out separately and the details are omitted from the formal proof. However, if a sequence is more complicated and finding a suitable $N(\\varepsilon)$ is non-trivial, you might consider including *some* of the details of how $N$ is found in the formal proof.\n- Notice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra when writing the full proof. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea.\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186199615 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence diverges to infinity since it's a positive geometric sequence with common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac97 > 1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n = \\infty\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\nM = 8\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that $a_n$ for $n={\\color{red}{63}}$ is smaller than $M = 8\\,000\\,000$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{\\color{red}{63}} = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^{\\color{red}{63}} \\approx 7\\,518\\,000 < M.\n$$\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}$, we have that $a_n$ is larger than $M= 8\\,000\\,000{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{64}|= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{64} \\approx 9666000 > M✓; a_{65}|= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{65} \\approx 12428000 > M✓; a_{66}|= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{66} \\approx 15978000 > M✓; ⋮\n$$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $M$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\nM = 15\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{66}}\n$$\n\nIndeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{65} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{65} \\approx 12428000 < M\\cdot \\\\\na_{66} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{66} \\approx 15978000 > M✓ \\\\\na_{67} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{67} \\approx 20544000 > M✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a sequence with a (positive) infinite limit is that *no matter how large* $M$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $M$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $M$, i.e.,\n$N= N(M)$.\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence diverges to infinity relies on finding $N(M)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons."] [17592186199618 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence once more:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\sqrt n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nLet's discuss how to construct a formal proof that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n = \\infty\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that this means the following (using formal notation):\n\n$$\n\\forall M > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: 2\\sqrt n \\gt M\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by writing this in words:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $M > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{n} > M$.\n\nEarlier in the lesson, we showed that for any $M > 0$, if we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$\n\nthen $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nUsing this result, we continue the proof:\n\n> *Suppose $M > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}$.*\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the lower bound for the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\sqrt{n} \\mid > 2\\sqrt{\\frac{M^{2}}{4}}; = 2 \\cdot \\frac{M}{2}; = M.\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(2\\sqrt n) = \\infty.\n$$*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down our formal proof."] [17592186199619 :tutorial/content "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(2\\sqrt n\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\n, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $M > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{n} > M$.\n> *Suppose $M > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}$.*\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $2\\sqrt{n} \\mid > 2\\sqrt{\\frac{M^{2}}{4}}; = M$.\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(2\\sqrt n) = \\infty.\n$$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof contains no details about how a suitable $N$ is found. This is usually worked out separately for simple sequences, and the details are omitted from the formal proof. However, if a sequence is more complicated and finding a suitable $N(M)$ is non-trivial, you might consider including *some* of the details of how $N$ is found in the formal proof.\n- Notice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra when writing the full proof. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea.\n\nLet's now see how to prove that a sequence diverges to negative infinity."] [17592186199620 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 2n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by assuming that the antecedent is true:\n\n> **Step 1:** *Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.*\n\nNow, we use the definitions of evenness and oddness.\n\n> **Step 2:** *Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $m + 2n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> **Step 3:** *Substituting $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ into the expression, we get*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle m + 2n|= (2a) + 2(2b + 1); = 2a + 4b + 2; = 2\\underbrace{(a + 2b + 1)}_{c}.\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=a + 2b + 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm + 2n = 2c\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m + 2n$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=a + 2b + 1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $m + 2n|= 2\\underbrace{(a + 2b + 1)}_{c}; = 2c$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> **Step 4:** *Therefore, $m + 2n$ is even.*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down the full proof."] [17592186199622 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:*\n> ***Case 1.*** *If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $n^{2} - 3n|= (2a)^{2} - 3(2a); = 4a^{2} - 6a; = 2(2a^{2} - 3a)$.\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - 3a$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means that it is even.*\n> ***Case 2.*** *If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.*\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle n^{2} - 3n|= (2a + 1)^{2} - 3(2a + 1); = 4a^{2} - 2a - 2; = 2(2a^{2} - a - 1).\n$$\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means it is even.*\n> *Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-3n$ is even for any integer $n$.*"] [17592186199625 :tutorial/content "You will have learned certain *divisibility tests* early in your mathematical career. One such rule is determining whether a number is divisible by $3$.\n\n> *An integer is divisible $3$ if and only if the sum of the digits is divisible by $3$.*\n\nFor example, we can use this rule to check that $2\\,387\\,465\\,652$ is divisible by $3$. The sum of the digits is given by\n\n$$\n2+3+8+7+4+6+5+6+5+2 = 48\n$$\n\nand $48$ is divisible by $3$. Therefore, $2\\,387\\,465\\,652$ is divisible by $3$.\n\nThese rules may have seemed mysterious when you first studied them. However, we can prove them using the properties of modular congruence!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's prove the \"is divisible by $3$\" rule.\n\nFirst, let $N$ be a positive integer. We'll express $N$ in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\nN = 10^{n}a_{n} + \\cdots + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_0\n$$\n\nHere, $a_{n}, \\ldots, a_2, a_{1}, a_{0}$ are the digits of the number.\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n3 \\mid N \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad 3 \\mid \\big(a_{n} + \\cdots + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_0 \\big)\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the statement in terms of congruences:\n\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $3 \\mid N \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad N \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nNow, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n> *Expressing $N$ in expanded form, we get*\n> $10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} \\mid ≡0 \\mid (mod3)$.\n\nNext, we need to show that this implies\n\n$$\na_{n} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_{0}≡0 \\mid (mod3)\n$$\n\nTo do this, recall the multiplication properties of modular arithmetic:\n\n- For any $k\\in \\mathbb N$, we have $a \\equiv b \\:\\Leftrightarrow\\: a^k\\equiv b^k \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\: n)$.\n- For any $c\\in \\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$, we have $a \\equiv b \\:\\Leftrightarrow\\: ac \\equiv bc \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\: n)$.\n\nWith this in mind, note that\n$10 \\equiv 1 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 10^k a_k \\equiv 1^k a_k \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\: 3)$.\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Now, without changing the congruence, we can swap each occurrence of $10$ with $1$ on the left-hand side since they're congruent modulo $3{:}$*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + 10^{2}a_{2} + 10a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3) \\\\ (1)^{n}a_{n} + ⋯ + (1)^{2}a_{2} + (1)a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3) \\\\ a_{n} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_{0} & ≡0 & (mod3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write down that conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation means that*\n> $3 \\mid \\big(a_{n} + \\cdots + a_{2} + a_{1} + a_0 \\big)$.\n\nAnd we're done!\n\n**Note:** All the transformations we performed above also work in reverse, which means that the right-to-left implication is also true.\n\nWe can also use modular congruence to prove slightly more complex divisibility rules. Let's see an example."] [17592186203665 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $J_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{KL}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $KJ_1 = \\dfrac12 KL$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 3 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL} = \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 4 | $L_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{JK}$ | Given |\n| 5 | $KL_1 = \\dfrac12 JK$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a midpoint}}$ |\n| 6 | $\\dfrac{KL_1}{JK} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 7 | $\\dfrac{KL_1}{KJ} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 8 | $\\angle{JKL} \\cong \\angle{L_1KJ_1}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 9 | $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\triangle{JKL} \\sim \\triangle{L_1KJ_1}}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 10 | $\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPSTP}}$ |\n| 11 | $\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 12 | $J_1L_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} JL$ | Multiplication principle |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 2 by $KL$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle KJ_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2}KL; \\frac{KJ_{1}}{KL}|= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{KL}{KL}; \\frac{KJ_{1}}{KL}|= \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From row 4, we have that $L_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{JK}$. Therefore, by the definition of a midpoint,\n$KL_1 = \\dfrac12 JK$.\n- Consider row 9. From rows 7 and 8, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{KL_1}{KJ} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}, \\qquad \\angle{JKL} \\cong \\angle{L_1KJ_1}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the SAS similarity criterion, $\\triangle{JKL} \\sim \\triangle{L_1KJ_1}$.\n- Consider row 10. From row 9, we have that triangles $\\triangle{JKL}$ and $\\triangle{L_1KJ_1}$ are similar. Corresponding parts of similar triangles are in proportion (CPSTP). Therefore, we have\n$\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$."] [17592186203666 :question/problem "Consider the following intervals.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A= (8,\\infty), \\qquad B = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nIf possible, construct a bijection from $A$ onto $B$. Use your result to determine whether the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186203667 :question/problem "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A= (-3,3), \\qquad B= \\mathbb{R}\n$$"] [17592186203672 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{5} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt[3]{5}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt[3]{5})^{3}|= \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{3}; 5|= \\frac{a^{3}}{b^{3}}; 5b^{3}|= a^{3},\n$$\n> which means that $5 \\mid a^3$.\n\nNow, recall the following property of divisibility by a prime $p{:}$\n$p \\mid xyz \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (p \\mid x) \\lor (p \\mid y) \\lor (p \\mid z)$\nApplying this property with $p = 5$ and\n\n$$\nx = y = z = a\n$$\n\nwe have\n$5 \\mid a^3 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a)$.\nSo, we have that $5 \\mid a$.\n\n> Since $5$ is prime, we have $5 \\mid a$, which means $a = 5c$ where $c$ is an integer.\n\nNow, we substitute $a = 5c$ into the equation $5b^3 = a^3$ and show that $5$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 5b^{3}|= (5c)^{3}; 5b^{3}|= 125c^{3}; b^{3}|= 25c^{3}; b^{3}|= 5 \\cdot 5c^{3}.\n$$\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $5 \\mid b^3$, which means that $5 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $5$, their greatest common divisor is at least $5$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $5 \\mid a$ and $5 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 5$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational."] [17592186203675 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\overbrace{\\frac{3b - a}{b}}^{c}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational."] [17592186203676 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contradiction of the following statement:\n\n> $\\sqrt{11}$ is irrational.\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\nAssume for a contradiction that $\\sqrt{11}$ $\\:\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{XX}}\\:$ rational.\n\nThen, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{11} = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{a}{b}\\dfrac{a}{b}}}\n$$\nfor some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{X}}$.\n\nBy squaring the above equation and applying some divisibility properties, we can show that $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) > 1$.\n\nBut this is a $\\:\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{contradiction}}\\:$ since $\\:\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{contradiction}}\\:$ by assumption.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\sqrt{11}$ $\\:\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{XX}}\\:$ rational."] [17592186203693 :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ defined over $X=\\{a, b, c, d, e\\}$ and $Y=\\{0,1\\}$, and the relation $S$ defined over $Y=\\{0,1\\}$ and $Z=\\{4, 7, 10\\}$, given below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle R|= {(a, 0), (a, 1), (c, 0), (d, 1), (e, 0), (e, 1)}; S|= {(1, 4), (1, 7), (1, 10)}\n$$\nExpress the composition $S \\circ R$ as a list of ordered pairs."] [17592186203713 :question/worked-solution "A $n$-ary relation $R$ over the sets $A_1,A_2,\\ldots,A_n$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A_1 \\times A_2 \\times \\cdots \\times A_n$.\n\n$$\nR \\subseteq A_1 \\times A_2 \\times \\cdots \\times A_n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $(A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4) \\in R \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})^4$ if and only if\n\n$$\nA_1 \\cup A_2 = A_3 \\cup A_4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $\\big(\\{1\\}, \\{3\\}, \\emptyset, \\{1,3\\}\\big) \\in R$ since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\{1\\} \\cup \\{3\\} = \\{1,3\\}= \\emptyset \\cup \\{1,3\\}.\n$$\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\big(\\mathbb{N}, \\emptyset, \\{2\\}, \\{2,3\\}\\big) \\not\\in R$ since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbb{N} \\cup \\emptyset = \\mathbb{N} \\neq \\{2,3\\} = \\{2\\} \\cup \\{2,3\\}.\n$$\n- Statement III is false. We have $\\big(\\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{4\\}\\big) \\not\\in R$ since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\{1\\} \\cup \\{2\\} = \\{1,2\\} \\neq \\{3,4\\} = \\{3\\} \\cup \\{4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186203714 :question/problem "Multiply the following integers in $\\mathbb Z_{10}$. Use the smallest nonnegative representative for the resulting class.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle [2] \\cdot [4]|= [?]; [4] \\cdot [9]|= [?]\n$$"] [17592186203715 :question/problem "Perform the following operations in $\\mathbb Z_{11}$. Use the smallest nonnegative representative for the resulting classes.\n$$\n\\displaystyle [9] + [5]|= [?]; [6] - [10]|= [?]\n$$"] [17592186203721 :question/problem "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n$$\n\\displaystyle A= (0,2), \\qquad B= (1,9)\n$$"] [17592186203722 :question/problem "Consider the following intervals.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A= (-4,\\infty), \\qquad B = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nIf possible, construct a bijection from $B$ onto $A$. Use your result to determine whether the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186203723 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{15} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt[3]{15}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt[3]{15})^{3}|= \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^{3}; 15|= \\frac{a^{3}}{b^{3}}; 15b^{3}|= a^{3},\n$$\n> which means that $15 \\mid a^3$.\n\nWe need to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\n> Let's use this to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\nTo do this, recall the following divisibility properties:\n\n- Transitivity of divisibility:\n$(x\\mid y) \\land (y\\mid z) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x\\mid z$\n- Divisibility of a power by a prime:\n$p \\mid a^n \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad p \\mid a$\n- Divisibility by a product:\n$(x\\mid z)\\land(y\\mid z)\\land(\\textrm{gcd}(x,y) = 1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad xy\\mid z$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n15=3\\cdot 5\n$$\n\nis the product of two primes, which suggests we may want to use the third property.\n\nAlso, if $15$ divides $a^3$, then $3$ and $5$ must divide $a^3$ by the first property.\n\nWe start by using the first property:\n\n> First, note that $15 = 3\\cdot 5$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(3∣15)∧(15∣a^{3})⇒3∣a^{3}$.\n\nNow, we use the second property:\n\n> Since $3$ is prime, this means that $3 \\mid a$.\n\nThe argument to show that $5\\mid a$ is identical, so we simply state that.\n\n> We also have $5 \\mid a$ by a similar argument.\n\nSince $3$ and $5$ are coprime, we can show that $15\\mid a$ by the third property.\n\n> Since $3\\mid a$ and $5\\mid a$, and $\\textrm{gcd(3,5)} =1$, we have that $15\\mid a$ which means that $a=15c$ for some integer $c$.\n\nNow, we substitute $a=15c$ into the equation $15b^3 = a^3$ and show that $15$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $15b^{3}|= (15c)^{3}; 15b^{3}|= 3, 375c^{3}; b^{3}|= 225c^{3}$.\n\nThen, we use the first property again:\n\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $225 \\mid b^3$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(15∣225)∧(225∣b^{3})⇒15∣b^{3}$,\n> which means that $15 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $15$, their greatest common divisor is at least $15$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $15 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 15$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational."] [17592186203724 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (\\overbrace{2a}^{c})/(b).\n$$\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational."] [17592186203726 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{9^{m+2}\\:: \\: m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{3^n \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}\n$$\nProve that $A \\subseteq B$."] [17592186203727 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{x \\in \\Bbb R: \\cos x = 1 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{x \\in \\Bbb R: \\sin x = 0\\}\n$$\nDisprove that $B \\subseteq A$."] [17592186203728 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{3m+1 \\:: \\: m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{3n+7 \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}\n$$\nProve that $A = B$."] [17592186203729 :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 24)$ if and only if $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$ and $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 8)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\cdot 3 + 8 \\cdot (-1) = \\textrm{gcd}(3,8)=1\n$$"] [17592186203729 :question/worked-solution "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> We need to show the following:\n> $$\n{ {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 24 )}} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big( { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 3 )}} \\quad \\land \\quad { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 8 )}} \\big)\n$$\n> This is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n{ {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 24 )}} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big( { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 3 )}} \\quad \\land \\quad { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 8 )}} \\big).\n$$\n\nTo do that, we first write the statement in terms of divisibility.\n\n> Or, in terms of divisibility,\n> $24 \\mid a-b \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(3 \\mid a-b \\quad \\land \\quad 8 \\mid a-b \\big)$.\n\nThen, we write $n=a-b$ to simplify the notation.\n\n> Let's denote $n=a-b$.\n\nSo, we now need to prove that\n\n$$\n24 \\mid n \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(3 \\mid n \\quad \\land \\quad 8 \\mid n \\big)\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n> If $24 \\mid n$, then $n =24k$ for some integer $k$. We can rewrite this equation as\n> $n = 3\\big(8k \\big)$,\n> which means that $3 \\mid n$. Also, we can write\n> $n = 8\\big(3k \\big)$,\n> which means that $8 \\mid n$. Therefore, $3 \\mid n$ and $8 \\mid n$.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n\\big( { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 3 )}} \\quad \\land \\quad { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 8 )}} \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad { {a \\equiv  b \\hspace{0.222em} ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 24 )}}.\n$$\n> Or, in terms of divisibility,\n> $\\big(3 \\mid a-b \\quad \\land \\quad 8 \\mid a-b \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 24 \\mid a-b$.\n\nAgain, we simplify the notation by introducing $n=a-b$.\n\n> Let's again denote $n=a-b$.\n\nSo now, we need to prove\n$\\big(3 \\mid n \\quad \\land \\quad 8 \\mid n \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 24 \\mid n$.\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $3 \\mid n$, then $n=3s$ for some integer $s$, and since $8 \\mid n$, then $n=8t$ for some integer $t$.\n\nNotice that the divisibility of $n$ by $3$ and $8$ are independent. So, in general, we should use different letters ($s$ and $t$) in the reasoning above.\n\nNext, we'll use the fact that our divisors are coprime.\n\n> The integers $3$ and $8$ are coprime and $3 \\cdot 3 + 8 \\cdot (-1) = 1$. So, multiplying this equation by $n$, we get\n> $3 \\cdot 3n + 8 \\cdot \\big(-n \\big) = n$.\n> Now, substituting $n=8t$ into the first term on the left-hand side and $n=3s$ into the second term, we get\n> $$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\cdot 3(8t) + 8 \\cdot (- (3s)) = n; 24 \\cdot (3t) + 24 \\cdot (-s) = n; 24 \\cdot (3t - s) = n,\n$$\n> which means that $24 \\mid n$. So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 24)$ if and only if $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$ and $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 8)$."] [17592186203730 :question/worked-solution "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n**Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nBy expanding the left-hand side and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2xy + y^{2} &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n2xy &= 0 \\\\\nx \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the left-hand side equals zero if $x=0$ or $y=0$.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x+y)^2= x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nHere, consider the two cases separately.\n\n> We have two subcases:\n> **Subcase 1.** Let $x=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (x + y)^{2}|= x^{2} + y^{2}; (0 + y)^{2}|= 0^{2} + y^{2}; y^{2}|= y^{2},\n$$\n> which is always true.\n\nAnd the second case.\n\n> **Subcase 2.** Let $y=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (x + y)^{2}|= x^{2} + y^{2}; (x + 0)^{2}|= x^{2} + 0^{2}; x^{2}|= x^{2},\n$$\n> which is always true.\n> Hence, if $x=0$ or $y=0$ then $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$ if and only if $x=0$ or $y=0$."] [17592186203731 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{x \\in \\Bbb Z: x \\equiv 3 \\: \\: (\\textrm{mod 4})\\}, \\qquad B = \\{x \\in \\Bbb Z: x \\equiv 1 \\: \\: (\\textrm{mod 2})\\}\n$$\nProve that $A \\subseteq B$."] [17592186203734 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the general terminology for sinusoidal curves:\n\n![](images/f6/f693f83ce4198b52d8b6dbca791cdf2855c7879dcf230193e824c23c10ede995.png)\n\nNow, we consider the given information:\n\n- In the formula, the amplitude equals $\\mid A \\mid = 2$. From this, we can find the minimum value:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 = \\dfrac{8-\\text{Min}}{2} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\text{Min} = 4 \\, \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n- The weight's lowest position is at $4\\, \\textrm{ft}$ and its highest position is at $8 \\, \\textrm{ft}$. This means that our graph must lie between the horizontal lines $D=4$ and $D=8$. Using this information, we can compute the midline (vertical shift):\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\dfrac{8+4}{2} = 6\n$$\n- At $t=0$, the weight is $6$ feet above the ground. This means that the point $(0,6)$ lies on the graph.\n- At $t= 1$ second, the weight is $8$ feet above the ground. This means that the point $(1,8)$ lies on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we can make the following sketch of the graph:\n\n![](images/83/832c9f84b2aaa27c7c724542c82781454a3bf2502eac3514473206e1f1d4da5b.png)\n\nFinally, let's find the constant $B$. The period of our function is\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot 4 = 4\n$$\n\nseconds. So,\n\n$$\nB = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\textrm{Period}} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{4} = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nD(t) = 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}2 \\right) + 6\n$$"] [17592186203738 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the conditions of the theorem:\n\n> We assume $\\gcd(a,n) = k > 1$. Therefore, by Bézout's identity, there exist $u,v \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $au+nv=k$.\n> Moreover, any number of the form\n> $au'+nv'$,\n> where $u'$ and $v'$ are integers, is a multiple of $k$.\n\nSince we are trying to prove a negation (i.e., that a solution does *not* exist), it's often easier to use proof by contradiction.\n\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Assume for a contradiction that $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a solution, which means that there exists $x_1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $ax_1 \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$.\n> Now, by the definition of integer congruence, there exists $c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle ax_1 - b = nc \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad ax_1 + (-nc) = b \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad ax_1 + n(-c) =b.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the results deduced from the conditions of the theorem.\n\n> Now, setting $x_1 = u'$ and $(-c) = v'$, we have that\n> $au'+nv' = b$,\n> which means $b$ must be a multiple of $k$.\n\nThis gives us our contradiction:\n\n> This is a contradiction to our assumption that $k \\not\\mid b$.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, the assumption that $ax \\equiv b \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n)$ has a solution is false, and we conclude that the congruence has no solution."] [17592186203739 :question/worked-solution "We start by using the conditions given in the theorem:\n\n> First, we assume by hypothesis that $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=k \\gt 1$ and $k \\mid b$, meaning that there exist $a_1,n_1,b_1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $a=a_1k, \\qquad n=n_1k, \\qquad b=b_1 k$.\n> Since $k$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $n$, the integers $a_1$ and $n_1$ must satisfy $\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=1$.\n\nNow, we use Bézout's identity:\n\n> Therefore, by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $a_1u+n_1v = 1$.\n\nWe rewrite the above equation as a congruence and use the fact that $n_1 \\equiv 0 \\, \\, (\\text{mod}\\:n_1){:}$\n\n> Since $n_1 \\equiv 0 \\, \\, (\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$, we can re-write this equation as the following congruence:\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}u + n_{1}v & ≡1 & (modn_{1}) \\\\ a_{1}u & ≡1 & (modn_{1})\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> This means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a_1$ (modulo $n_1$).\n\nNow, consider the congruence $a_1 \\cdot x \\equiv b_1\\: (\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$.\nSince\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=1\n$$\n\nthis congruence has the unique solution $x_1$ modulo $n_1$.\nWe can find this solution using the multiplicative inverse of $a_1$ found above.\n\n> So, multiplying both sides of the congruence $a_1x_1 \\equiv b_1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$ by $u$, we get\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}x_{1} & ≡b_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ u \\cdot a_{1}x_{1} & ≡u \\cdot b_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ (u \\cdot a_{1})x_{1} & ≡ub_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x_{1} & ≡ub_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ x_{1} & ≡b_{1}u & (modn_{1})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n> Consequently, $a_1 x_1 \\equiv b_1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$ has a unique solution $x_1=b_1u$ modulo $n_1$, and $x_1$ is also a solution of the original linear congruence.\n\nGiven a solution $x_1$ of our congruence, we can construct the other solutions based on $x_1$.\n\n> Therefore, all the solutions of the original congruence must be congruent to $x_1$ modulo $n_1$.\n> Let us now consider for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, k \\}$, the following expression:\n> $x_i = x_1 + (i-1)\\cdot n_1$\n> Note that indexes larger than $k$ will lead to repeating solutions modulo $n=n_1k$.\n\nNow, we show that these are solutions of the original congruence.\n\n> We will prove that $x_i$ is a solution of the original linear congruence for each $1 \\leq i \\leq k$. Indeed,\n> $$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrlrlr}\\displaystyle \\phantom{\\leftrightarrow} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} {a}_{1} {x}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} {a}_{1} ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} k {a}_{1} ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  k {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} k {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} a ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  b & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} n ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} a {x}_{i} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  b & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} n )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to show that the solutions found are all distinct:\n\n> These values form distinct solutions, meaning they are mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> Indeed, if $i$ and $j$ are indices from $\\{1,2,\\ldots, k-1\\}$ and $i \\neq j$, then, by assuming for a contradiction that $x_i \\equiv x_j \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$, we get the following:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}x_{i} & ≡x_{j} & (modn) \\\\ x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1} & ≡x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1} & (modn) \\\\ (i - 1)n_{1} & ≡(j - 1)n_{1} & (modn_{1}k) \\\\ (i - 1) & ≡(j - 1) & (modk) \\\\ i & ≡j & (modk)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n> Since both values are smaller than $k$, the final congruence means that $i=j$, which is a contradiction.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, our congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nThe full proof is stated below:\n\n> First, we assume by hypothesis that $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=k \\gt 1$ and $k \\mid b$, meaning that there exist $a_1,n_1,b_1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $a=a_1k, \\qquad n=n_1k, \\qquad b=b_1 k$.\n> Since $k$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $n$, the integers $a_1$ and $n_1$ must satisfy $\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=1$.\n> Therefore, by Bézout's identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $a_1u+n_1v = 1$.\n> Since $n_1 \\equiv 0 \\, \\, (\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$, we can re-write this equation as the following congruence:\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}u + n_{1}v & ≡1 & (modn_{1}) \\\\ a_{1}u & ≡1 & (modn_{1})\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> This means that $u$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a_1$ (modulo $n_1$).\n> So, multiplying both sides of the congruence $a_1x_1 \\equiv b_1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$ by $u$, we get\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}x_{1} & ≡b_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ u \\cdot a_{1}x_{1} & ≡u \\cdot b_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ (u \\cdot a_{1})x_{1} & ≡ub_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ 1 \\cdot x_{1} & ≡ub_{1} & (modn_{1}) \\\\ x_{1} & ≡b_{1}u & (modn_{1})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n> Consequently, $a_1 x_1 \\equiv b_1 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:n_1)$ has a unique solution $x_1=b_1u$ modulo $n_1$, and $x_1$ is also a solution of the original linear congruence.\n> Therefore, all the solutions of the original congruence must be congruent to $x_1$ modulo $n_1$.\n> Let us now consider for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, k \\}$, the following expression:\n> $x_i = x_1 + (i-1)\\cdot n_1$\n> Note that indexes larger than $k$ will lead to repeating solutions modulo $n=n_1k$.\n> We will prove that $x_i$ is a solution of the original linear congruence for each $1 \\leq i \\leq k$. Indeed,\n> $$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrlrlr}\\displaystyle \\phantom{\\leftrightarrow} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} {a}_{1} {x}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} {a}_{1} ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} k {a}_{1} ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  k {b}_{1} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} k {n}_{1} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} a ( {x}_{1} + ( i - 1 ) {n}_{1} ) & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  b & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} n ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} a {x}_{i} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  b & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} n )\\end{array}}\n$$\n> These values form distinct solutions, meaning they are mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> Indeed, if $i$ and $j$ are indices from $\\{1,2,\\ldots, k-1\\}$ and $i \\neq j$, then, by assuming for a contradiction that $x_i \\equiv x_j \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$, we get the following:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}x_{i} & ≡x_{j} & (modn) \\\\ x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1} & ≡x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1} & (modn) \\\\ (i - 1)n_{1} & ≡(j - 1)n_{1} & (modn_{1}k) \\\\ (i - 1) & ≡(j - 1) & (modk) \\\\ i & ≡j & (modk)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n> Since both values are smaller than $k$, the final congruence means that $i=j$, which is a contradiction.\n> Therefore, our congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$."] [17592186203740 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n- both populations have the same unknown variance.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_1} + \\dfrac{1}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $S_p^2$ is the pooled variance, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n_1+n_2-2$ degrees of freedom\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 2.3 - 1.8 = 0.5\n$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.04) = 0.025$.![](images/fa/fa232b7c2b3d8141f3d68a9c450e75e6c27fbdaba52682e01b47332f17859a85.png)\nAs a result,\n$t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2} = 2.04$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle E = 2.04 \\cdot 1.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{17} + \\dfrac{1}{15}} \\approx 1.084\n$$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$0.5 ± 1.084$"] [17592186203741 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n- both populations have the same unknown variance.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n$(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2} \\cdot S_p \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_1} + \\dfrac{1}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $S_p^2$ is the pooled variance, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(T > t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n_1+n_2-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 2.17 - 1.95 = 0.22\n$$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005.\n$$\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.72) = 0.005$. As a result,\n$t_{n_1+n_2-2, \\alpha/2} = 2.72$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$$\n\\displaystyle E = 2.72 \\cdot 0.11 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{21} + \\dfrac{1}{17}} \\approx 0.098\n$$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$0.22 ± 0.098$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.22 \\pm 0.098$.\n- Statement II is false. $0.2$ lies inside the confidence interval:\n$(0.22 - 0.098, \\, 0.22 + 0.098) = (0.122, \\,0.318)$\n- Statement III is false. Since not all values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are higher than $0.2$, there is no evidence to suggest the difference between the means is $0.2$ or higher.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186203761 :question/worked-solution "The diagram corresponding to our automaton is shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c79f07e5e39ef11f5366fadd2ad37478a925c2296b498f647553c099e4572de.png)\n\nLet's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Consider the string $0001{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 0001)|= δ(δ(p, 0), 001); = δ(q, 001); = δ(δ(q, 0), 01); = δ(r, 01); = δ(δ(r, 0), 1); = δ(p, 1); = p\n$$![](images/5b/5bdbfd194560c41eb304d9bf489623ab0f82e1bb28b24cac9573f599afe017b2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $p$. Since $p$ is not a final (accepting) state, the string $0001$ is rejected.\n- Consider the string $1010{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 1010)|= δ(δ(p, 1), 010); = δ(p, 010); = δ(δ(p, 0), 10); = δ(q, 10); = δ(δ(q, 1), 0); = δ(q, 0); = r\n$$![](images/7d/7d75e94dd273fc37034baefc52234aad4056f528ec5824ddf850e729d44931c0.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $1010$ is accepted.\n- Consider the string $0011{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 0011)|= δ(δ(p, 0), 011); = δ(q, 011); = δ(δ(q, 0), 11); = δ(r, 11); = δ(δ(r, 1), 1); = δ(q, 1); = q\n$$![](images/a4/a497ef18d08c2cbefcf7dc7164d3073f2b64851c1124fb4f22e573804570d6d2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $q$. Since $q$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0011$ is accepted.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186203766 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the one-factor ANOVA table for comparing $K$ groups in a sample of size $n$ gives the following data.\n\n| Source | $\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$ | $\\,df\\,$ | $\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$ | $\\,F\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Between Groups | $\\text{SSB}$ | $K-1$ | $\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$ | $\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$ |\n| Within Groups | $\\text{SSW}$ | $n-K$ | $\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$ |  |\n| Total | $\\text{SST}$ | $n-1$ |  |  |\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$.\n\nLet's go through the missing entries in the given table.\n\n- $\\text{SSB}$ can be computed using the above relation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle SSB|= SST - SSW; = 7.6 - 4.15; = 3.45\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{B}|= K - 1; = 3 - 1; = 2.\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{W}|= n - K; = 26 - 3; = 23.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSB|= \\frac{SSB}{K - 1}; = \\frac{3.45}{3 - 1}; = 1.725; \\approx 1.73.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSW|= \\frac{SSW}{n - K}; = \\frac{4.15}{26 - 3}; = 0.180434…; \\approx 0.18.\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{1.725}{0.180\\,434\\ldots} \\approx 9.56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n| Source | $\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$ | $\\,df\\,$ | $\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$ | $\\,F\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Between | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3.45}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.73}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9.56}$ |\n| Within | $4.15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.18}$ |  |\n| Total | $7.6$ | $25$ |  |  |"] [17592186203769 :question/problem "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left.\\lef\\right|.\\left._(5\\right| \\mid _(0) \\mid _(0)\\right|_(3); 7\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}6; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ [math]\\phantom{4}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{7}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}4 & [math]\\phantom{0}[/math] & 3\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$"] [17592186203775 :question/worked-solution "![](images/de/def8939d5b6a390496bbf83b2b5d595980c27189c4cbfea3af1e492dba4688b9.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $r \\perp s$ | Given |\n| 2 | $m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 3 | $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 5 | $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 6 | $m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 7 | $90^\\circ = m\\angle b$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 8 | $m\\angle a = m\\angle b$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 9 | $\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 6. From row 5, we have that $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $90^\\circ$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 90^{∘} + m∠b|= 180^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 180^{∘} - 90^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 90^{∘}; m∠b|= 90^{∘}\n$$\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ have the same measures. Therefore, by definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent."] [17592186203777 :question/worked-solution "![](images/2b/2b6d992c05a2e3286d5c8a7fc57b8d59e67f2faac291817d22ef5ba515877b6b.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 2 | $AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Pythagorean theorem for}\\triangle ACD}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 4 | $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\\triangle DCB$ |\n| 5 | $\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of bisector}}$ |\n| 6 | $CB = AC$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $DB^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $AC^2 + DC^2 = DB^2$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 9 | $AD^2 = DB^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 10 | $AD = DB$ | Algebra |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, we have that $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$. Then, by the Pythagorean theorem applied to $\\triangle ACD$, we have\n$AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$.\n- Consider row 5. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a bisector, we have\n$\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$.\n- Consider row 7. From rows 6 and 4, we have that $CB = AC$ and $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$$\n\\displaystyle DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2 = AC^2 + DC^2.\n$$\n- Consider row 9. From rows 2 and 8, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}AD^2} = AC^2 + DC^2 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AC^2 + DC^2 = {\\color{blue}DB^2}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}AD^2} = {\\color{blue}DB^2}$."] [17592186203778 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 8 | $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of perpendicular bisector}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 10 | $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS criterion}}$ |\n| 11 | $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We're given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. Then, by the definition of perpendicular lines, the angles formed by the intersection of $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ have a measure of $90^\\circ$. So, we have that $m\\angle{DCB} = 90^\\circ$.\n2. Notice that $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n3. From row 2, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ are supplementary. Therefore, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ by the definition of supplementary angles.\n4. From row 3, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle{DCB}$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠DCB + m∠DCA|= 180^{∘}; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCA|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB\n$$\n5. From rows 1 and 4, $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ and $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle DCA\n\n= 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ\n= 90^\\circ.\n$$\n6. From rows 1 and 5, we have $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ = m\\angle DCA$. Therefore, by the transitive property of equality,\n$m\\angle{DCB} = m\\angle{DCA}$.\n7. From row 6, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $m\\angle{DCA}$ have the same measure. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the definition of congruent angles.\n8. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a perpendicular bisector, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.\n9. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Then, $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ by the reflexive property of congruence.\n10. From rows 8, 7, and 9, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}, \\qquad \\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA, \\qquad \\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nTherefore, by the SAS criterion, $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$.\n11. From row 10, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}B}} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}A}}$. Then, $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ by CPCTC."] [17592186203781 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 2 | $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 3 | $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 4 | $\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary | Linear pair postulate |\n| 5 | $m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 6 | $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$ | Given |\n| 7 | $m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 8 | $(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$ | Substitution |\n| 9 | $m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$ | Addition principle |\n| 10 | $\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 11 | $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 3. From row 2, we have $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle FHD$ from both sides\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠CHF + m∠FHD|= 180^{∘}; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD.\n$$\n- Consider row 7. From row 3, we have that $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$, and from row 6, we have that $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m \\angle EGB= 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$\n- Consider row 11. From row 10, we have that $\\angle BGH\\cong \\angle FHD$. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, if two lines and a transversal form corresponding angles that are congruent, then the lines are parallel. Notice that $\\angle BGH$ and $\\angle FHD$ are corresponding angles formed by the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ with the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel."] [17592186203782 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle 1\\cong \\angle 3$ | Vertical angles theorem |\n| 2 | $\\angle 7$ and $\\angle 8$ are supplementary angles | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle 7 + m\\angle 8 = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 8$ are supplementary angles | Given |\n| 5 | $m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 8 = 180^\\circ$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 6 | $180^\\circ = m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 8$ | Symmetric property of congruence |\n| 7 | $m\\angle 7 + m\\angle 8 = m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 8$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 8 | $m\\angle 7 = m\\angle 1$ | Addition principle |\n| 9 | $\\angle 7 \\cong \\angle 1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruence}}$ |\n| 10 | $\\angle 7 \\cong \\angle 3$ | Transitive property of congruence |\n| 11 | $l$ and $m$ are parallel lines | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle 7$ and $\\angle 8$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle{7}$ and $\\angle{8}$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 7. From rows 3 and 6, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{m\\angle 7 + m\\angle 8}} = 180^\\circ \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad 180^\\circ = {\\color{blue}{m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 8}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we have\n${\\color{blue}{m\\angle 7 + m\\angle 8}} = {\\color{blue}{m\\angle 1 + m\\angle 8}}$.\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, we have that $m\\angle 7 = m\\angle 1$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent:\n$\\angle 7 \\cong \\angle 1$\n- Consider row $11$. From row $10$, we have $\\angle 7 \\cong \\angle 3$. Since these are corresponding angles, by the converse corresponding angles postulate, the lines $l$ and $m$ are parallel."] [17592186203786 :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a5a3369700ea3b63a1c5cc41e3ab35dc2837ba43d27b05f99fb18f0e2dde432.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $L$ and $M$ are the midpoints of $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{BC}$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{BC}{BM} = 2, \\qquad \\dfrac{AB}{BL} = 2.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $\\overline{ML} \\parallel \\overline{AC}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete it by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{ABC} \\cong \\angle{LBM}$ |  |\n| 2 | $\\dfrac{BC}{BM} = 2\\,$ and $\\,\\dfrac{AB}{BL} = 2$ |  |\n| 3 | $\\dfrac{BC}{BM} = \\dfrac{AB}{BL}$ |  |\n| 4 | $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{LBM}$ |  |\n| 5 | $\\angle{BAC} \\cong \\angle{BLM}$ |  |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AC} \\parallel \\overline{ML}$ |  |"] [17592186203786 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\angle{ABC} \\cong \\angle{LBM}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\dfrac{BC}{BM} = 2\\,$ and $\\,\\dfrac{AB}{BL} = 2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 3 | $\\dfrac{BC}{BM} = \\dfrac{AB}{BL}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 4 | $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{LBM}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS similarity criterion}}$ |\n| 5 | $\\angle{BAC} \\cong \\angle{BLM}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles in similar triangles}}$ |\n| 6 | $\\overline{AC} \\parallel \\overline{ML}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. Notice that $\\angle ABC$ and $\\angle LBM$ represent the same angle. Therefore, by the reflexive property of congruence, we have $\\angle{ABC} \\cong \\angle{LBM}$.\n2. We're given that $\\dfrac{BC}{BM} = 2$ and $\\dfrac{AB}{BL} = 2$.\n3. From row 2, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{BC}{BM}}} = 2 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad 2 = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{AB}{BL}}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{BC}{BM}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{AB}{BL}}}.\n$$\n4. From rows 3 and 1, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{BC}{BM} = \\dfrac{AB}{BL}, \\qquad \\angle{ABC} \\cong \\angle{LBM}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the SAS similarity criterion, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{LBM}$.\n5. From row 4, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{LBM}$. Therefore,\n$\\angle{BAC} \\cong \\angle{BLM}$\nsince they're corresponding angles in similar triangles.\n6. Finally, notice that $\\angle{BAC}$ and $\\angle{BLM}$ are corresponding angles. Furthermore, they are congruent. Therefore, by the converse corresponding angles postulate,\n$\\overline{AC} \\parallel \\overline{ML}$."] [17592186203789 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b591f4229ded0738dd9f0fa56ef35e7b90c7f14be9dddd3e45393b1d0e35b0f.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\overline{RJ}$, $\\overline{SI}$, and $\\overline{TL}$ are the medians of $\\triangle{RST}$ and $G$ is its centroid. Prove that\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_G = \\dfrac{x_R+x_S+x_T}{3}, \\qquad y_G = \\dfrac{y_R+y_S+y_T}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186203789 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $J$ in terms of the coordinates of $S$ and $T{:}$\n\n> Since $\\overline{RJ}$ is a median, we have that $J$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{ST}$. So, if $(x_J,y_J)$ are the coordinates of $J$, by the\n> midpoint formula, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{J} = \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}, y_{J} = \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we write the coordinates of the centroid using the formula for partitioning a line segment in a given ratio:\n\n> By the centroid ratio theorem, the point $G$ splits the median $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$, starting from the point $R$. Now, by partitioning the segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in a given ratio, we obtain that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{G}|= \\frac{x_{R} + 2x_{J}}{3}; = \\frac{x_{R} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}}{3}; = \\frac{x_{R} + x_{S} + x_{T}}{3},; y_{G}|= \\frac{y_{R} + 2y_{J}}{3}; = \\frac{y_{R} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}}{3}; = \\frac{y_{R} + y_{S} + y_{T}}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186203791 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $PB = \\dfrac12{PQ}$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 4 | $\\dfrac{PB}{PQ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Multiplication principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}\n$$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles in similar triangles}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{BA} \\parallel \\overline{RQ}$ | Converse corresponding angles postulate |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 4. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 3 by $PQ$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle PB|= \\frac{1}{2}PQ; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ}; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ}; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From rows 1 and 4, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = \\dfrac 12 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\dfrac 12 = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\n- Consider row 6. Notice that $\\angle RPQ$ and $\\angle APB$ represent the same angle. Therefore, by the reflexive property of congruence, we have $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$.\n- Consider row 8. From row 7, we have\n$\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$.\nTherefore, we have that $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ since they're corresponding angles in similar triangles."] [17592186203798 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\dfrac{AB}{PQ} =2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\dfrac{AC}{PR}=2$ | Substitution |\n| 3 | $\\dfrac{AB}{PQ} =\\dfrac{AC}{PR}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 4 | $\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{P}$ | Given |\n| 5 | $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{PQR}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\textrm{SAS similarity criterion}}$ |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. From the diagram, we have $AB=4a$ and $PQ=2a$. Thus, by substitution, we get that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{AB}{PQ}=\\dfrac{4a}{2a}=2.\n$$\n- Consider row 3. From rows 1 and 2, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{AB}{PQ} = 2, \\qquad \\dfrac{AC}{PR}=2.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality\n$\\dfrac{AB}{PQ} = \\dfrac{AC}{PR}$.\n- Consider row 5. In rows 3 and 4, we are given that the angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{P}$ are congruent and that their corresponding sides are proportional. Therefore, $\\triangle{ABC} \\sim \\triangle{PQR}$ by the SAS similarity criterion."] [17592186203800 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{15}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{15} x_i^2 = 9.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$."] [17592186203801 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim U(\\theta, \\theta+6)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter and $\\theta>0$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, x_3\\ldots,x_{5}$ you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{5} x_i^2 = 140.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$."] [17592186203806 :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $5$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\, x_i \\,$** | $10$ | $9$ | $8$ | $8$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\, y_i \\,$** | $15$ | $12$ | $14$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^5 x_i^2 = 334,\n$$ calculate $S_{xx}.$"] [17592186203806 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations $x_{1}, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n}$ and $y_{1}, y_2, \\ldots, y_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data $x_i$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the $x_i$ data:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = 10 + 9 + 8 + 8 + 5 = 40.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^5 x_i^2 = 334.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\nS_{xx} =334 - \\dfrac{1}{5}(40)^2 = 334- 320 = 14.\n$$"] [17592186203810 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{12}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i^2 = 360.\n$$\n\nCalculate the method of moments estimate for $\\theta$."] [17592186203811 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{15}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Geom}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{15} x_i^2 = 30.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$. Round your final answer to three decimal places."] [17592186203812 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{7}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{7}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{7} x_i = \\dfrac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$."] [17592186203813 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim B(3, \\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2,\\ldots, x_{6}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{6} x_i^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$."] [17592186203814 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{10}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\mu, \\, \\sigma^2)$, where $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are unknown parameters. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 70, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 550.\n$$\n\nFind the method of moments estimates $\\widehat{\\mu}_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ for $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, respectively."] [17592186203816 :question/worked-solution "We have a sample with the following information:\n\n- The sample size is $n = 100$\n- The number of groups is $K = 8$\n\nLet's calculate the degrees of freedom for each of the sums of squares:\n\n- For the total sum of squares $(\\textrm{SST})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{T}|= n - 1; = 100 - 1; = 99.\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares between the groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{B}|= K - 1; = 8 - 1; = 7.\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares within the groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{W}|= n - K; = 100 - 8; = 92.\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ndf_T = df_B + df_W\n$$"] [17592186203817 :question/worked-solution "The sum of squares within groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$ and sum of squares between groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} \\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2, \\qquad \\text{SSB} = \\sum_{k=1}^K n_k \\left(\\overline{x}_k - \\overline{x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $K$ is the number of groups,\n- $n_k$ is the number of individuals in the $k$th group,\n- $x_{jk}$ is the $j$th data point from the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}_{k}$ is the sample mean of the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}$ is the grand mean.\n\nNow, if $s_k^2$ is the unbiased estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$ obtained from the data in group $k$ only, then\n\n$$\ns_k^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n_k-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n_k-1)s_k^2 = \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThis means we can express $\\text{SSW}$ in the following simplified form:\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K (n_k - 1)s_k^2\n$$\n\nMoreover, the total sum of squares is\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (n-1)s^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the grand sample size,\n- $s^2$ is the grand (unbiased) estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn our case, since there are $3$ groups, we have $K=3$, and we can compute SSW as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSSW &= (20 - 1)(3.2)^{2} + (24 - 1)(3.4)^{2} + (20 - 1)(3.8)^{2} \\\\\n&= 194.56 + 265.88 + 274.36 \\\\\n&= 734.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\nn = 20+24+20 = 64\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (64-1)\\cdot (15)=945\n$$\n\nNow, let's now examine our statements:\n\n- Computing the given ratio, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{\\text{SST}} = \\dfrac{734.8}{945}\\approx \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}78\\%}.\n$$\n- We're interested in determining the between-group variability. First, recall that\n$\\textrm{SSW} + \\textrm{SSB} = \\textrm{SST}$\nand therefore\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSW}}{\\textrm{SST}} + \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSB}}{\\textrm{SST}} = 1.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{SSB}{SST}|= 1 - \\frac{SSW}{SST}; \\approx 100% - 78%; = 22%; < 40%\n$$\nSince a low proportion of the variability is *between* groups, relatively high variability is attributable to differences *within* the group means. In other words, this suggests the between-group variability is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{low}}$."] [17592186203818 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_n = 2a_{n-1} + n + 1, \\qquad a_1=0, \\quad n \\geq 2.\n$$\nFind the solution of this recurrence relation for $n \\geq 1$."] [17592186203824 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_n = 6a_{n-1} - 6^n, \\qquad a_1=12, \\quad n \\geq 2.\n$$\nFind the solution of this recurrence relation for $n\\geq1$."] [17592186203855 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 1 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 2 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe directed weighted graph with vertices $v_1$, $v_2$, $v_3$ (in this exact order) has the adjacency matrix shown above. Draw the graph that corresponds to this adjacency matrix."] [17592186203865 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{1}}{\\partial  {x}_{1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{1}}{\\partial  {x}_{2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{1}}{\\partial  {x}_{n}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{2}}{\\partial  {x}_{1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{2}}{\\partial  {x}_{2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{2}}{\\partial  {x}_{n}}} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{m}}{\\partial  {x}_{1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{m}}{\\partial  {x}_{2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {f}_{m}}{\\partial  {x}_{n}}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{x} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4},\n$$ we get\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 27\\end{array} \\right]} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} + 2 {x}_{2} + 4 {x}_{3} + 8 {x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{1} + 3 {x}_{2} + 9 {x}_{3} + 27 {x}_{4}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have $f_1(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 + 2x_2 + 4x_3 + 8x_4$ and $f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 + 3x_2 + 9x_3 +27x_4.$ So, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{4}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{4}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}$ gives the same matrix."] [17592186203866 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}   \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $$\n\\displaystyle L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}),\n$$ where\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad \\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at \n$$\n\\mathbf{A}  =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}?\n$$"] [17592186203869 :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf y: \\mathbb R^3\\to \\mathbb R^2$ and $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^2\\to \\mathbb R^2,$ defined by\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} - {x}_{2} {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} + {x}_{2} {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} + {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} - {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhat is \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))\n$$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_0 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}?\n$$"] [17592186203869 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{f}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{y}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{y}_0 = \\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}_0) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 - 1 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) \\\\ 0 + 1 \\cdot  ( - 1 )\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ we get\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\bigg|_{\\mathbf{y}_0} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus, the total derivative of $\\mathbf{y}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -{x}_{3} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ we get\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\bigg|_{\\mathbf{x}_0} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & - 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203870 :question/problem "For $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y, \\mathbf z\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x)))\n$$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}?$"] [17592186203870 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{f}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{z} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{C}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{z}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf z} = \\mathbf{C}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{z}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{z}(\\mathbf{y}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {z}_{1}}{\\partial  {y}_{1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {z}_{1}}{\\partial  {y}_{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {z}_{2}}{\\partial  {y}_{1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  {z}_{2}}{\\partial  {y}_{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{y}_0 = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}_0 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & - 1\\end{array} \\right]} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ we get\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf z}{\\textrm d \\mathbf y} \\bigg|_{\\mathbf{y}_0} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ - 1 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf{y}$ acts on a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ by multiplying it by $\\mathbf{A}$ and outputs a vector $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d\\mathbf y}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = \\mathbf{A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203873 :question/problem "Consider the functions $\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{y},$ where $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^3, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ and the matrices $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ are given by\n$$\n\\mathbf{A} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & - 3 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{B} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 0 \\\\ - 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhat is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} \\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf x))\n$$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0 = [1, \\, 0, \\, -2]^T?$"] [17592186204199 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (0, 2), B = R\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: A → B$, defined as\n$2\\cot (x)$ {{field-1}}\nis a\n{{field-2}} of $A$ onto $B$, we have that {{field-3}}."] [17592186204200 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (1, ∞), B = R\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: B → A$, defined as\n$e^{x + 1}$ {{field-1}}\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}."] [17592186204201 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (0, 3), B = (0, 2)\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: A → B$, defined as\n$\\frac{3}{2}x$ {{field-1}}\nis a {{field-2}} of $A$ onto $B$, we have that {{field-3}}."] [17592186204202 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (-1, ∞), B = R\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: A → B$, defined as\n$\\ln (x - 1)$ {{field-1}}\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}."] [17592186204203 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (-1, 1), B = R\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: B → A$, defined as\n$\\frac{π}{2}\\arctan (x)$ {{field-1}}\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}."] [17592186204204 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = R, B = (-1, ∞)\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: A → B$, defined as\n$e^{x - 1}$ {{field-1}}\nis a {{field-2}} of $A$ onto $B$, we have that {{field-3}}."] [17592186204205 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = R, B = (\\sqrt{2}, ∞)\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: B → A$, defined as\n$\\ln (x + \\sqrt{2})$ {{field-1}}\nis a {{field-2}} of $B$ onto $A$, we have that {{field-3}}."] [17592186204206 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = (0, 1), B = (-3, 5)\n$$\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function $f: A → B$, defined as\n$3x$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nis a {{field-3}} of $A$ onto $B$, we have that {{field-4}}."] [17592186204245 :question/problem "Consider the following relations defined on $X = {1, 2, 3, 4}$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle R|= {(1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 3), (3, 4)}; S|= {(2, 1), (3, 1), (3, 2)}\n$$\nExpress the composition $S∘R$ as a list of ordered pairs.$S∘R = {(1, 1), (1$,\n{{field-1}} $), ($\n{{field-2}} $, 1), ($\n{{field-3}} $, 2)}$"] [17592186204294 :question/problem "Consider the following relations defined on $R$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle R|= {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y = \\ln x}; S|= {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x = y^{2}}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following pairs are elements of the relation $S∘R$?\n\n1. $(1, 0)$\n2. $(e^{2}, \\sqrt{2})$\n3. $(e^{2},-\\sqrt{2})$\n4. $(e^{-1},-1)$"] [17592186204304 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {12m + 36: m ∈ Z}, B = {4n: n ∈ Z}\n$$\nProve that $A⊆B$.\n\nTo prove that $A⊆B$, we take an arbitrary element of {{field-1}} and show that it lies in {{field-2}}."] [17592186204305 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {12m + 36: m ∈ Z}, B = {4n: n ∈ Z}\n$$\nDisprove that $B⊆A$.\n\nWe need to show that {{field-1}}."] [17592186204308 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {4^{m}: m ∈ Z}, B = {2^{n}: n ∈ Z}\n$$\nProve that $A⊆B$.\n\nTo prove that $A⊆B$, we take an arbitrary element of {{field-1}} and show that it lies in {{field-2}}."] [17592186204309 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {x ∈ Z: x≡2(mod 9)}, B = {x ∈ Z: x≡2(mod 3)}\n$$\nProve that $A⊆B$.\n\nTo prove that $A⊆B$, we take an arbitrary element of {{field-1}} and show that it lies in {{field-2}}."] [17592186204310 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {x ∈ Z: x≡2(mod 9)}, B = {x ∈ Z: x≡2(mod 3)}\n$$\nDisprove that $B⊆A$.\n\nWe need to show that {{field-1}}."] [17592186204315 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {2 \\cdot 4^{m}: m ∈ Z}, B = {2^{n}: n = 2k + 1, k ∈ Z}\n$$\nProve that $A = B$.\n\nTo prove that $A = B$, we must show that $A⊆$ {{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}\n{{field-3}}\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186204316 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {x ∈ R: \\sin (x + 2π) =-1}, B = {x ∈ R: \\sin x =-1}\n$$\nProve that $A = B$.\n\nTo prove that $A = B$, we must show that $A⊆$ {{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}\n{{field-3}}\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186204318 :question/problem "Consider the following sets.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = {x ∈ R: \\tan x = 1}, B = {x ∈ R: 2\\sin^{2} x = 1}\n$$\nProve that $A⊆B$.\n\nTo prove that $A⊆B$, we take an arbitrary element of {{field-1}} and show that it lies in {{field-2}}."] [17592186204346 :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $35∣n$ if and only if $5∣n$ and $7∣n$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5 \\cdot 3 + 7 \\cdot (-2) = gcd(5, 7) = 1\n$$\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n$⇒$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThis is the same as proving two {{field-3}}."] [17592186204347 :question/problem "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $a≡b(mod15)$ if and only if $a≡b(mod3)$ and $a≡b(mod5)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot 2 = gcd(3, 5) = 1\n$$\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n$⇐$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nThis is the same as proving two {{field-3}}."] [17592186204371 :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ac210bacf21b40718a376da57ab411d889fc74483b92426e6751656f76c9020.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}\n$$"] [17592186204372 :question/problem "![](images/34/3479f994d1e9b77e0b70e91f2606282d70e6cca3ffc83d90fe56766fd2795871.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}\n$$"] [17592186204408 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6b6c5c7c79189fa446c1ef25cc3c93a2c76bbb27e096083a268fc83c20e118f.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $X_{1}$ and $Z_{1}$ are the midpoints of $YZ^(―)$ and $XY^(―)$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n$\\frac{YZ_{1}}{YX} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $\\frac{X_{1}Z_{1}}{XZ} = \\frac{1}{2}$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of this statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $X_{1}$ is the midpoint of $YZ^(―)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| 2 | $YX_{1} = \\frac{1}{2}YZ$ | {{field-